1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,194
Lesson 8.5.

2
00:00:02,574 --> 00:00:03,978
Thermodynamic K-FAC.

3
00:00:05,083 --> 00:00:08,637
K-FAC's bottleneck is
inverting each layer's

4
00:00:08,637 --> 00:00:12,594
factors, which costs n³
for a layer of n neurons.

5
00:00:12,974 --> 00:00:16,442
In twenty twenty-five, Donatella
and colleagues proposed

6
00:00:16,442 --> 00:00:19,600
doing those inversions on
a thermodynamic computer.

7
00:00:19,980 --> 00:00:24,681
The idea is that of unit six applied
to each layer, with an important

8
00:00:24,681 --> 00:00:29,246
practical detail: how many bits
the device's input and output need.

9
00:00:30,350 --> 00:00:33,183
The GPU computes, as usual, the activation

10
00:00:33,183 --> 00:00:35,747
and gradient statistics of each layer.

11
00:00:36,127 --> 00:00:41,022
Instead of inverting the factors
digitally, it loads them into the device,

12
00:00:41,022 --> 00:00:45,784
which solves A x = b systems of size
n with Ornstein-Uhlenbeck dynamics.

13
00:00:46,164 --> 00:00:51,849
The result returns to the GPU,
which applies the step G⁻¹ ∇W A⁻¹.

14
00:00:52,229 --> 00:00:57,296
The moving averages of the factors can
live on the GPU or on the device itself.

15
00:00:58,417 --> 00:01:04,210
Per layer and per iteration,
digital K-FAC costs O(b n² + n³).

16
00:01:04,590 --> 00:01:09,457
The thermodynamic version
costs O(b n² + n² κ²), where

17
00:01:09,457 --> 00:01:13,514
κ bounds the condition
number of the factors,

18
00:01:13,894 --> 00:01:18,052
and without moving averages
in memory, only O(b n) memory.

19
00:01:18,432 --> 00:01:21,368
The advantage grows
linearly with the network

20
00:01:21,368 --> 00:01:23,979
width: the cubic term becomes quadratic.

21
00:01:24,359 --> 00:01:29,155
With the layers of modern transformers,
thousands of neurons wide, that matters.

22
00:01:30,283 --> 00:01:31,797
How much is gained in practice?

23
00:01:32,177 --> 00:01:36,620
The authors measured what fraction
of time K-FAC spends inverting on

24
00:01:36,620 --> 00:01:40,996
AlgoPerf benchmark tasks: eleven
percent on a vision transformer on

25
00:01:40,996 --> 00:01:45,373
ImageNet, and twenty-seven percent
on a graph network on molecules.

26
00:01:45,753 --> 00:01:49,930
If that part becomes almost free,
they estimate reaching the same

27
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:54,170
validation metric about fifty
percent faster on the graph network.

28
00:01:54,550 --> 00:01:59,205
Note: speeding up only twenty-seven
percent of the time allows, by Amdahl's

29
00:01:59,205 --> 00:02:03,612
law, at most a thirty-seven percent
gain with the same number of steps,

30
00:02:03,992 --> 00:02:06,690
so that estimate must
also include changes in

31
00:02:06,690 --> 00:02:09,268
the steps; it is worth
reading the details.

32
00:02:09,648 --> 00:02:13,104
These are estimates from
digital measurements and

33
00:02:13,104 --> 00:02:16,911
simulations: no physical
device ran these experiments.

34
00:02:18,017 --> 00:02:22,336
The device receives the factors through
digital-to-analog converters and

35
00:02:22,336 --> 00:02:26,776
delivers the solution through
analog-to-digital converters, with few bits.

36
00:02:27,156 --> 00:02:31,829
A naive quantizer can break the
positivity of the factors; the authors

37
00:02:31,829 --> 00:02:35,835
use a conservative, diagonal-dominant
one that preserves it.

38
00:02:36,214 --> 00:02:40,966
Training a ResNet, they found that input
quantization has little effect, but

39
00:02:40,966 --> 00:02:45,718
output quantization matters: at least
eight output bits are needed, and with

40
00:02:45,718 --> 00:02:49,407
eight bits K-FAC already competes
with full-precision Adam.

41
00:02:50,517 --> 00:02:52,289
A methodological warning.

42
00:02:52,669 --> 00:02:57,169
In our softmax layer we measured a
single step: with a naive uniform

43
00:02:57,169 --> 00:03:01,405
quantizer, the input is what hurts
most, with a cosine of 0.7 at

44
00:03:01,405 --> 00:03:05,045
four bits, and the output
barely changes the direction.

45
00:03:05,425 --> 00:03:09,629
It is the opposite of what the authors
found in full training, with their

46
00:03:09,629 --> 00:03:13,892
conservative quantizer and errors that
accumulate over thousands of steps.

47
00:03:14,272 --> 00:03:19,754
The lesson: single-step metrics are no
substitute for training experiments.

48
00:03:20,883 --> 00:03:24,352
Use it in wide networks, where
the cubic inversion dominates.

49
00:03:24,732 --> 00:03:29,109
Before promising speedups, measure what
fraction of time goes into inverting.

50
00:03:29,489 --> 00:03:34,349
And design with a conservative input
quantizer and at least eight output bits.

51
00:03:34,729 --> 00:03:38,260
In lab eleven you will
sweep input and output bits.

52
00:03:39,383 --> 00:03:42,430
Common mistakes: promising
a total speedup when

53
00:03:42,430 --> 00:03:45,087
inversion is only a fraction of the time.

54
00:03:45,467 --> 00:03:47,820
Ignoring the squared condition factor.

55
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:51,341
Using naive quantizers
that break positivity.

56
00:03:51,721 --> 00:03:55,206
And judging the precision
needed with single-step metrics.

57
00:03:56,317 --> 00:04:02,209
In summary: thermodynamic K-FAC replaces
each layer's cubic inversions with

58
00:04:02,209 --> 00:04:07,630
analog solves costing n² κ², with
an advantage that grows with width.

59
00:04:08,010 --> 00:04:11,271
The speedups are estimated,
not measured on hardware.

60
00:04:11,651 --> 00:04:14,734
And precision matters:
input with a conservative

61
00:04:14,734 --> 00:04:17,368
quantizer and at least eight output bits.

62
00:04:18,483 --> 00:04:22,420
To practice: compute the
cost ratio for a wide layer.

63
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:25,650
Apply Amdahl's law to
the inversion fraction.

64
00:04:26,030 --> 00:04:29,589
Explain how a uniform
quantizer breaks positivity.

65
00:04:29,969 --> 00:04:34,588
And explain why output errors weigh
more in training than in one step.
